-- Network+ (N10-009) Domain 5 (Network Troubleshooting) expansion.
-- Adds questions n-q-700..n-q-758 (59) and flashcards n-f-700..n-f-771 (72),
-- topping up objectives 5.1-5.5 to 33 items each.
--   5.1 Troubleshooting methodology ................. 14 q + 16 f
--   5.2 Cabling & physical interface issues ......... 13 q + 16 f
--   5.3 Network performance issues .................. 11 q + 13 f
--   5.4 Tools & protocols for network troubleshooting 14 q + 17 f  (previously 2 q, 0 f)
--   5.5 General networking issues ...................  7 q + 10 f

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('n-q-700','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',1,
 $q$A technician has gathered information from the user, duplicated the reported error, and confirmed nothing else has changed recently. According to the troubleshooting methodology, what should the technician do next?$q$,
 $q$["Implement the solution immediately", "Establish a theory of probable cause", "Document the resolution", "Escalate to the vendor"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Once the problem has been identified (gathering info, duplicating it, checking for recent changes), the next stage is forming a theory of probable cause. Implementing a fix or documenting are later stages, and escalation is only warranted once theories have been tried and exhausted, not before one has even been formed.$q$),
('n-q-701','netplus',5,'5.1','multi',2,
 $q$Which TWO of the following activities belong to the "identify the problem" stage of the troubleshooting methodology?$q$,
 $q$["Establish a plan of action to implement the fix", "Question the user and gather information about the symptoms", "Determine if anything has changed recently", "Document the outcome for future reference"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Gathering information from the user and determining what has changed are both part of identifying the problem before a cause is even theorized. Establishing a plan of action happens after a theory is tested and confirmed, and documentation is the final step, after the fix is verified.$q$),
('n-q-702','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',2,
 $q$A technician tests their theory that a failed patch cable is causing a user's outage by swapping in a known-good cable, and connectivity is restored. What should the technician do next?$q$,
 $q$["Establish a new theory of probable cause", "Escalate the ticket to a senior technician", "Establish a plan of action and implement the solution", "Close the ticket without further action"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$Confirming the theory means the technician can move on to planning and implementing the full fix (for example, properly replacing and labeling the cable). A new theory is only needed if the test had failed to confirm the cause, escalation is for when the cause can't be determined, and closing without implementing or verifying skips required steps.$q$),
('n-q-703','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',2,
 $q$A technician's theory that a misconfigured DNS server is causing name-resolution failures is not confirmed after testing on an affected host. What should the technician do next?$q$,
 $q$["Implement the DNS fix anyway since it was the most likely cause", "Skip ahead to documenting the incident as resolved", "Tell the user the problem cannot be fixed", "Re-establish a new theory or escalate if the cause cannot be determined"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$When a theory isn't confirmed, the technician returns to forming a new theory, or escalates to someone with broader knowledge or access if their own theories are exhausted. Implementing an unconfirmed fix or declaring the issue unresolvable skips proper diagnosis.$q$),
('n-q-704','netplus',5,'5.1','ordering',1,
 $q$Order these four stages of the troubleshooting methodology from first to last.$q$,
 $q$["Test the theory to determine the cause", "Identify the problem", "Establish a plan of action and implement the solution", "Establish a theory of probable cause"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 3, 0, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$This is the standard order used throughout CompTIA's methodology: identify the problem, establish a theory of probable cause, test the theory, then establish a plan of action and implement the solution - followed later by verification and documentation.$q$),
('n-q-705','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',1,
 $q$What is the primary purpose of the "establish a plan of action and identify potential effects" step, performed after a theory has been confirmed?$q$,
 $q$["To anticipate side effects or downtime the fix might cause before implementing it", "To gather the initial symptoms from the affected user", "To decide whether the problem is worth fixing at all", "To write the final report for management"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$This step exists so a confirmed fix isn't applied blindly - the technician considers what else the change might impact (other services, users, scheduled maintenance windows) before implementing it. Gathering symptoms happens earlier, during problem identification, and final reporting happens during documentation.$q$),
('n-q-706','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',3,
 $q$After implementing a fix for a reported printing failure, a technician confirms the printer works again and closes the ticket. Which required step of the methodology did the technician skip?$q$,
 $q$["Establishing a theory of probable cause", "Verifying full system functionality and, if applicable, implementing preventive measures", "Testing the theory to determine the cause", "Questioning the user about symptoms"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Confirming that the one reported symptom is gone isn't the same as verifying full system functionality - the fix could have affected other devices, or the same root cause could be impacting other users. Preventive measures are added at this stage too, before the ticket is closed. The other listed steps happen earlier in the process and were implicitly already completed to reach a working fix.$q$),
('n-q-707','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',2,
 $q$Several users on different floors report intermittent connectivity at roughly the same time, but with different symptoms each is describing. What approach should the technician take?$q$,
 $q$["Assume all reports describe the exact same root cause and apply one fix everywhere", "Close all but the first ticket as duplicates", "Treat each report as its own problem and investigate individually before assuming a single shared cause", "Ignore reports that don't match the first one investigated"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$The methodology calls for approaching multiple problems individually, since seemingly related symptoms may have separate, unrelated causes. Assuming one shared cause or dismissing dissimilar reports risks missing an actual distinct problem.$q$),
('n-q-708','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',2,
 $q$During the "establish a theory of probable cause" stage, why should a technician question the obvious before considering more complex explanations?$q$,
 $q$["Obvious causes are never actually correct", "Skipping obvious checks makes the technician appear more skilled", "CompTIA requires it purely as a formality with no diagnostic value", "Simple causes, like an unplugged cable or a disabled port, are common and quick to rule out before investigating deeper issues"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$Ruling out simple, common causes first is efficient - many outages trace back to something basic like a loose cable or a disabled port, and confirming or eliminating that early saves time versus jumping straight to complex theories.$q$),
('n-q-709','netplus',5,'5.1','ordering',2,
 $q$A tested theory turns out to be incorrect. Order the technician's next four actions from first to last.$q$,
 $q$["Form a new theory of probable cause based on additional information", "Once confirmed, establish a plan of action to implement the fix", "Test the theory and find it is not confirmed", "Test the new theory"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 0, 3, 1]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$When a tested theory turns out to be wrong, the methodology loops back to forming a new theory that incorporates whatever was learned, testing that new theory, and only once it is confirmed moving on to planning and implementing the fix - it does not skip ahead to implementation on an unconfirmed theory.$q$),
('n-q-710','netplus',5,'5.1','multi',3,
 $q$A technician chooses a "divide and conquer" approach to test a theory about a failing web application. Which TWO actions are consistent with that approach?$q$,
 $q$["Checking an OSI layer in the middle of the stack first, such as verifying IP connectivity, to quickly narrow whether the fault is above or below that point", "Starting at the physical layer and checking every layer in order up to the application", "Based on the result, focusing further testing on only the layers above or below the layer just checked", "Starting at the application layer and checking every layer in order down to the physical"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Divide and conquer starts in the middle of the OSI stack, often at the network layer, and uses the result to eliminate either everything above or everything below, narrowing the search quickly. Checking every layer in strict order from the bottom up or the top down describes the bottom-to-top and top-to-bottom approaches instead, not divide and conquer.$q$),
('n-q-711','netplus',5,'5.1','ordering',3,
 $q$A user reports they cannot reach an internal file server. Order the technician's six actions from first to last, following the troubleshooting methodology.$q$,
 $q$["Schedule and apply the permanent fix to the firewall rule during a low-impact window", "Ask the user what they were doing when the problem started and confirm the symptom by trying to reach the server themselves", "Record the cause, the fix, and the outcome in the ticketing system", "Temporarily revert the firewall rule change on a test system to see if access is restored", "Confirm other users can now reach the server and monitor for recurrence", "Theorize that a recently changed firewall rule is blocking the connection"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 5, 3, 0, 4, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$This follows identify (ask the user and duplicate the symptom), theorize (a recent firewall change), test (revert the rule on a test system), plan and implement (schedule and apply the real fix), verify (confirm access and watch for recurrence), and document (record it) - the full methodology in sequence.$q$),
('n-q-712','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',2,
 $q$A technician has tested two different theories for a network outage and both were disproven. No further ideas come to mind, and other technicians on shift have none either. What should happen next?$q$,
 $q$["Escalate the issue to someone with more experience or broader access and knowledge", "Continue guessing at random theories until one works", "Tell the user the network cannot be fixed", "Skip to documentation without a resolution"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$Escalation exists specifically for when a technician's theories are exhausted and the cause still isn't known - it isn't a failure, it's the correct next step to bring in someone with more expertise, access, or vendor contacts. Random guessing wastes time, and declaring the issue unfixable or writing it off without an actual resolution abandons the process.$q$),
('n-q-713','netplus',5,'5.1','ordering',2,
 $q$An issue could not be resolved by the on-shift technician and required outside help. Order these four actions from first to last.$q$,
 $q$["Receive an updated theory and fix from the escalation contact", "Test two independent theories without success", "Verify functionality and document the resolution", "Escalate to a technician with deeper system access"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 3, 0, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Escalation happens only after theories have genuinely been tested and failed, not before; after the escalation contact identifies the cause and fix, the original technician's responsibility continues through verification and documentation, since closing out the ticket properly doesn't disappear just because someone else found the root cause.$q$),
('n-q-714','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',1,
 $q$A newly run patch cable shows a solid green link light on both the switch and the NIC, yet file transfers across it are extremely slow with frequent retransmissions. What does this indicate?$q$,
 $q$["The cable is definitely fine and the problem must be the application", "The link light only confirms physical connectivity and successful negotiation - it does not guarantee error-free performance", "A lit link light always means the cable is functioning at full speed with no errors", "The switch port is administratively disabled"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$A link light shows that the physical link came up and duplex/speed negotiation succeeded, but it says nothing about ongoing error rates - a marginal cable that is near its length limit, damaged, or poorly terminated can still light up while causing enough attenuation or crosstalk to force retransmissions. A disabled port would not show a link light at all.$q$),
('n-q-715','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,
 $q$During cable termination, a technician left an unusually long section of each wire pair untwisted before crimping the connector. Which physical-layer problem is this likely to cause?$q$,
 $q$["Attenuation", "A duplicate IP address", "Crosstalk", "A duplex mismatch"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$The twists in each pair cancel out electromagnetic coupling between pairs; untwisting too much wire before termination gives adjacent pairs room to induce crosstalk onto each other. Attenuation is signal loss over distance, not a termination-technique issue, and IP conflicts and duplex mismatches are unrelated to physical wiring.$q$),
('n-q-716','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,
 $q$A cable run was installed directly alongside fluorescent light ballasts and inside the same conduit as electrical power wiring. Users on that run experience intermittent errors that a cable tester cannot explain. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["A duplicate IP address on the segment", "An incorrect subnet mask", "DNS server misconfiguration", "Electromagnetic interference (EMI) from the nearby electrical sources"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$Fluorescent ballasts and power wiring radiate electromagnetic energy that can couple onto nearby copper data cabling, causing intermittent errors that a simple continuity/wiremap test won't catch. The fix is rerouting the cable away from the interference source or using shielded cable. IP addressing, subnet, and DNS issues would not correlate with proximity to electrical equipment.$q$),
('n-q-717','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',3,
 $q$A cable tester reports that all eight pins show continuity end-to-end with no opens or shorts, yet the link performs poorly with frequent errors. What wiring fault is consistent with this result?$q$,
 $q$["A split pair, where wires from two different twisted pairs were connected to pins that a simple continuity test treats as correct", "A fully severed conductor", "An unplugged connector", "A completely reversed T568A/T568B wiring on one end with no continuity at all"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$A split pair uses wires from two different physical pairs to carry a single signal pair's pins - continuity is intact end-to-end, so a basic tester reports success, but because the wires no longer belong to the same twisted pair, the noise cancellation that twisting provides is lost, causing crosstalk-driven errors. A severed conductor, unplugged connector, or fully reversed wiring would show up as an open or a continuity failure, not a passing test.$q$),
('n-q-718','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,
 $q$A technician connects two older switches that both use fixed MDI ports with no auto-MDI-X, using a standard straight-through cable, and the link light never comes on. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["The cable exceeds the maximum supported length", "The transmit and receive pairs need to be crossed, requiring a crossover cable between two MDI devices", "One switch has a duplicate IP address", "The switches are on different VLANs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Connecting two like devices, such as switch-to-switch, both using fixed MDI ports, requires a crossover cable so each side's transmit pair reaches the other side's receive pair; a straight-through cable leaves transmit aligned with transmit, so the link never negotiates. Auto-MDI-X ports would compensate automatically, but older fixed-MDI switches do not. Cable length, IP addressing, and VLANs would not prevent a link light from appearing at all.$q$),
('n-q-719','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',3,
 $q$A fiber run is bent tightly around a sharp corner to fit inside a cable tray and shows elevated signal loss that worsens whenever the tray is bumped. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["The fiber connector is the wrong physical type", "The transceiver is set to half duplex", "The cable's minimum bend radius has been exceeded, causing increased attenuation at the bend", "The fiber is affected by electromagnetic interference"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$Bending fiber tighter than its rated minimum bend radius stresses the glass core, increasing attenuation at that point, and physical disturbance to an already-stressed bend can worsen it further, matching the bump-sensitive symptom. Fiber is immune to EMI, and duplex or connector-type mismatches would not produce loss that varies with physical disturbance to the cable.$q$),
('n-q-720','netplus',5,'5.2','multi',2,
 $q$Which TWO of the following are physical-layer causes that can produce a link which stays up but performs poorly, rather than failing to connect at all?$q$,
 $q$["A completely unplugged patch cable", "Attenuation from a cable run that is close to or beyond its rated maximum length", "An incorrect DNS server address configured on the host", "Crosstalk from poor termination or cable damage"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 3]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Attenuation and crosstalk both degrade signal quality without necessarily preventing link negotiation, so the link light stays on while throughput and error rates suffer. An unplugged cable prevents the link from coming up at all, and a DNS misconfiguration is a name-resolution issue with no physical-layer symptom.$q$),
('n-q-721','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',1,
 $q$A gigabit-rated switch port connects to a NIC over an old Category 5 cable rated only for 100 Mbps operation. What is the most likely result?$q$,
 $q$["The link will not come up at all under any circumstances", "The cable will physically overheat and fail", "The switch will assign a duplicate IP address", "The link negotiates down to a lower supported speed such as 100 Mbps"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$When the cable can't reliably support gigabit signaling, auto-negotiation typically falls back to a speed the cable can handle, such as 100 Mbps, rather than refusing to link entirely. Cables don't overheat from a category mismatch, and cable category has no relationship to IP addressing.$q$),
('n-q-722','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',1,
 $q$A desk cable frequently disconnects whenever someone bumps the desk, and inspection shows the connector's retention clip is cracked and no longer seats fully in the jack. What is the cause?$q$,
 $q$["A damaged connector", "Attenuation", "A duplex mismatch", "DHCP scope exhaustion"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$A cracked retention clip can't hold the connector firmly seated, so physical vibration or bumps interrupt contact - a mechanical, damaged-connector problem. Attenuation and duplex mismatch are electrical or negotiation issues that wouldn't correlate with the connector physically loosening, and DHCP exhaustion is unrelated to a single desk's physical connection.$q$),
('n-q-723','netplus',5,'5.2','multi',3,
 $q$A switch port is fixed at full duplex while the connected NIC is fixed at half duplex. Which TWO symptoms would this duplex mismatch typically produce?$q$,
 $q$["Late collisions reported on the half-duplex side", "The link light fails to illuminate at all", "The host receives an APIPA (169.254.x.x) address", "Noticeably degraded throughput despite a link light being present"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 3]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$A duplex mismatch still allows the link to come up and negotiate a speed, so the link light illuminates - but the half-duplex side detects collisions as late collisions when the full-duplex side transmits during what it thinks is a shared medium, and overall throughput suffers. A missing link light or an APIPA address point to a different failure entirely, such as no physical link or a DHCP failure, not a duplex mismatch.$q$),
('n-q-724','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,
 $q$A fiber link that recently worked fine now shows steadily increasing errors, and inspecting the connector end-face under a fiber scope reveals visible dust and scratches. What should the technician do?$q$,
 $q$["Replace the switch, since dust cannot affect a fiber connection", "Clean the connector end-face with an approved fiber-cleaning tool before further troubleshooting", "Increase the transceiver's transmit power setting", "Change the VLAN assigned to that port"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Dust and scratches on a fiber end-face scatter and block light, directly increasing attenuation and errors - cleaning it with a proper fiber-cleaning tool is the correct first step, since it's cheap, fast, and a very common real-world cause of degraded fiber links. Dust absolutely can affect a fiber connection, and neither a VLAN change nor blindly raising transmit power addresses a dirty connector.$q$),
('n-q-725','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',3,
 $q$A technician installs a single-mode SFP transceiver into a switch port connected via multimode fiber to a matching single-mode SFP at the far end. The link fails to establish. What is the most likely explanation?$q$,
 $q$["The switch port is configured for the wrong VLAN", "The SFPs are experiencing a duplex mismatch", "The fiber type, multimode, does not match the transceivers' single-mode design, causing excessive loss or no light coupling", "The Ethernet cable exceeds the 100-meter copper limit"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$Single-mode transceivers are designed to couple light into the small core of single-mode fiber; running them over multimode fiber, which has a much larger core and different light propagation, causes severe coupling loss or link failure, independent of matching transceivers on both ends. VLANs, duplex settings, and copper length limits do not apply to a fiber-type and transceiver mismatch.$q$),
('n-q-726','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,
 $q$A user's connection to the switch shows a lit link light, but the port's activity LED shows no activity at all even while the user is actively transferring a large file. What does this suggest?$q$,
 $q$["The cable is definitely broken and must be replaced immediately", "The switch itself has completely failed", "The port is misconfigured for the wrong duplex setting", "The physical link and negotiation succeeded, but no actual frames are currently traversing that specific port, pointing away from a cabling fault and toward something further up the stack, such as an incorrect VLAN or a host-side block"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$A link light with no activity during an active transfer means the physical connection is fine but frames aren't reaching that port - so the fault is more likely logical, such as a VLAN assignment, a host-side firewall or NIC issue, or the wrong switch port entirely, rather than physical cabling. If the cable were broken, the link light itself would typically go out.$q$),
('n-q-727','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',1,
 $q$Large file transfers to a shared drive take noticeably longer during business hours and return to normal speed overnight. What does this pattern most strongly suggest?$q$,
 $q$["Bandwidth saturation from other traffic competing for the same link during peak usage", "A failing network cable", "A DNS misconfiguration", "An IP address conflict"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$A slowdown that correlates with the time of day, and clears up after hours, points to contention for limited bandwidth rather than a hardware or configuration fault - a failing cable, DNS issue, or IP conflict would not typically improve simply because fewer people are using the network.$q$),
('n-q-728','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',2,
 $q$Ping response times to the same server vary widely between tests - sometimes 5 ms, sometimes 300 ms - though no requests time out. What is this variability called?$q$,
 $q$["Packet loss", "Jitter", "Attenuation", "A duplicate IP address"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Jitter is variation in latency between packets; here every ping gets a reply, so there is no packet loss, but the delay is inconsistent, which is the defining symptom of jitter and is especially disruptive to real-time traffic like voice and video. Attenuation and IP conflicts are physical or addressing issues, not timing variability.$q$),
('n-q-729','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',1,
 $q$Pings to a remote server consistently return around 180 ms with no timeouts, on every test at every time of day. What does this indicate?$q$,
 $q$["Packet loss", "Jitter", "Consistently high latency, likely from distance or routing rather than an intermittent fault", "A duplex mismatch"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$A steady, repeatable response time, even if high, describes latency, not jitter, which requires variability, or packet loss, which requires missing replies. Consistently high latency over distance is often simply a function of the physical or routing path rather than a fault to fix.$q$),
('n-q-730','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',3,
 $q$A video stream repeatedly freezes and shows compression artifacts, and a continuous ping to the same server shows several requests receiving no reply at all. What is occurring?$q$,
 $q$["Jitter", "Attenuation", "DNS resolution failure", "Packet loss"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$Packets that never receive a reply, rather than replies that are simply delayed, indicate packet loss, which causes exactly this kind of freezing and artifacting in a video stream as data is dropped along the path. Jitter would still return every reply, just at inconsistent intervals, and attenuation or DNS problems are unrelated to mid-stream loss on an already-established connection.$q$),
('n-q-731','netplus',5,'5.3','multi',2,
 $q$A single switch uplink carrying traffic for an entire floor is saturated during peak hours, slowing every user on that floor. Which TWO conditions would make this uplink a bottleneck?$q$,
 $q$["Every access port on the floor is individually rated faster than the clients connected to it", "The floor's switch is running the latest firmware version", "The uplink is the same speed as, or slower than, the aggregate traffic generated by all the access ports feeding into it", "No link aggregation or higher-speed uplink has been provisioned despite growing floor traffic"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 3]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$A bottleneck forms when a single link's capacity is less than the combined demand funneling into it - an uplink sized the same as or slower than the aggregate access-port traffic, without added capacity such as aggregation or a faster uplink as demand grows, is the classic cause. Individual access ports being fast enough, or the switch running current firmware, don't address the shared uplink's total capacity.$q$),
('n-q-732','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',2,
 $q$In a densely populated office, several nearby wireless access points are all configured to the same 2.4GHz channel. Users report slow, inconsistent Wi-Fi despite strong signal strength. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["Co-channel interference from overlapping access points competing for the same channel", "Wireless encryption is misconfigured", "The access points have a hardware failure", "An IP address conflict between the access points"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$Multiple access points sharing one channel in close proximity must take turns transmitting, causing contention and slowdowns even when signal strength is fine - the fix is spreading access points across non-overlapping channels, such as 1, 6, and 11 on 2.4GHz. Encryption settings, hardware failure, and IP conflicts wouldn't produce this specific channel-contention pattern.$q$),
('n-q-733','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',3,
 $q$A conference room access point shows excellent signal strength to every connected device, but Wi-Fi performance degrades severely whenever more than 30 laptops join during a large meeting. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["Attenuation from distance to the access point", "Client and channel density - too many devices contending for limited airtime on that access point", "A duplicate IP address among the laptops", "A cable fault on the access point's uplink"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Even with strong signal, a single access point has finite airtime to share among all associated clients; a large number of simultaneous devices causes contention and collisions on the wireless medium itself, degrading performance for everyone. Distance-based attenuation wouldn't explain uniformly strong signal readings, and IP conflicts or a wired uplink fault wouldn't specifically appear only at high device counts.$q$),
('n-q-734','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',1,
 $q$Devices near the far corner of a large warehouse frequently lose their wireless connection or show very weak signal, while devices closer to the access point work fine. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["A duplicate IP address", "DNS server failure", "Insufficient wireless coverage - the area is too far from the nearest access point", "Co-channel interference"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$Signal weakening and drops that correlate specifically with distance from the access point point to a coverage gap - the fix is typically adding another access point or a repeater to extend coverage into that area. IP conflicts, DNS failures, and channel interference wouldn't produce symptoms tied so directly to physical distance.$q$),
('n-q-735','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',2,
 $q$Wi-Fi users near a break room report brief but repeated slowdowns that occur only while the microwave oven is running. What is the most likely explanation?$q$,
 $q$["A DHCP scope exhaustion issue", "A misconfigured default gateway", "An Ethernet duplex mismatch", "RF interference from the microwave oven on the overlapping 2.4GHz frequency range"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$Microwave ovens, along with some cordless phones and Bluetooth devices, operate in the same general frequency range as 2.4GHz Wi-Fi and can leak enough RF energy to disrupt nearby wireless traffic while running - moving to the 5GHz band avoids this shared spectrum. DHCP, gateway, and wired-duplex issues would not correlate with the microwave's operation.$q$),
('n-q-736','netplus',5,'5.3','multi',3,
 $q$Which TWO statements correctly distinguish latency from jitter?$q$,
 $q$["Jitter always means packets are being dropped entirely", "Latency is the time a packet takes to reach its destination; jitter is the variation in that time between packets", "High but perfectly consistent delay is a latency problem, not a jitter problem", "Latency can only be caused by wireless interference, never by wired links"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Latency measures delay itself; jitter measures how much that delay varies from packet to packet - so a link can have high latency with zero jitter if the delay is consistent, or low latency with high jitter if it's erratic. Jitter is about timing variation, not dropped packets, since that is packet loss, and latency occurs on wired links too, from distance, queuing, and processing delay.$q$),
('n-q-737','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',3,
 $q$A branch office's WAN link shows 0% packet loss and stable, low variation in response times, but every response is around 220 ms due to the physical distance to the data center. Video calls over this link feel laggy. What does this scenario illustrate?$q$,
 $q$["Consistently high latency from propagation distance, which is not the same problem as packet loss or jitter and may not be fixable by troubleshooting the link itself", "A packet loss problem that requires replacing the WAN circuit", "A jitter problem caused by inconsistent queuing", "A duplex mismatch on the WAN router's interface"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$Stable response times with 0% loss rule out jitter and packet loss - the laggy feel comes purely from propagation delay over distance, a latency characteristic of the physical or geographic path that isn't a fault to repair the way a broken cable or misconfigured device would be; mitigations focus on reducing sensitivity to latency, such as better codecs or edge servers, rather than fixing the link. A duplex mismatch would show collisions or errors, which aren't described here.$q$),
('n-q-738','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',1,
 $q$A user can successfully ping a server's IP address, but pinging the server's hostname fails entirely. What category of problem does this point to?$q$,
 $q$["A physical cabling problem", "A DNS resolution problem", "A duplex mismatch", "An exhausted DHCP scope"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Since the IP-based ping already proves basic network connectivity works, a hostname-based ping failing means the name simply isn't resolving to an address - a DNS issue, not a physical, duplex, or DHCP problem, all of which would also affect the IP-based ping.$q$),
('n-q-739','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',2,
 $q$nslookup returns an old IP address for a server that was migrated to a new address days ago, even though other users on the network already see the new address correctly. What is the most likely cause on the affected machine?$q$,
 $q$["A duplicate IP address conflict", "A duplex mismatch on the NIC", "A stale entry in the local DNS resolver cache", "An incorrect subnet mask"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$If other machines already resolve correctly, the DNS record itself is fine - the affected machine is most likely serving a cached answer from before the change. Clearing the local resolver cache, for example with ipconfig /flushdns, forces a fresh lookup. A duplicate IP, duplex mismatch, or subnet mask error would not cause a stale name-to-IP mapping specifically on one host.$q$),
('n-q-740','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',1,
 $q$A traceroute to a destination shows request timeouts at one intermediate hop, but every hop after it, including the final destination, responds normally. What does this most likely indicate?$q$,
 $q$["The network is completely down beyond that hop", "The destination server is offline", "The local machine has a DNS problem", "That intermediate router is simply configured not to respond to the traceroute probes, not necessarily a fault"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$Many routers are configured to deprioritize or ignore the ICMP or UDP probes traceroute uses, without dropping actual traffic - a single silent hop with a fully responsive path afterward is a normal, common pattern, not proof of an outage. If the network were down beyond that hop, later hops and the destination wouldn't respond at all.$q$),
('n-q-741','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',1,
 $q$A technician needs to inspect the full contents and header fields of packets exchanged during a failing application handshake to determine exactly where the failure occurs. Which tool is designed for this?$q$,
 $q$["A protocol analyzer such as Wireshark, or tcpdump for command-line capture", "ipconfig", "arp -a", "A cable tester"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$A protocol analyzer or packet capture tool decodes the actual bytes on the wire, including headers and payloads, letting a technician see exactly where a handshake breaks down. ipconfig only shows local configuration, arp -a shows local IP-to-MAC mappings, and a cable tester only checks physical wiring - none of them show packet contents.$q$),
('n-q-742','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',2,
 $q$A technician wants to discover every live host and open service port across an entire subnet before an audit. Which tool is designed for this?$q$,
 $q$["ping", "nmap", "netstat", "ipconfig"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$nmap is a network scanner built to sweep a range of addresses, identify live hosts, and enumerate open ports and services across many machines at once. ping only tests one address at a time, netstat reports connections on the local host only, and ipconfig shows only local configuration.$q$),
('n-q-743','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',2,
 $q$Which command displays a host's current IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway?$q$,
 $q$["netstat", "arp -a", "ipconfig (or ifconfig/ip on Linux/macOS)", "nslookup"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$ipconfig, or ifconfig/ip on Linux and macOS, reports the local interface's IP configuration, including address, mask, and gateway. netstat shows connections and ports, arp -a shows IP-to-MAC mappings, and nslookup queries DNS - none of them display the host's own IP configuration.$q$),
('n-q-744','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',2,
 $q$Two hosts on the same subnet are both configured with the same static IP address, and a technician wants to confirm which physical device currently holds that address by checking which MAC address a nearby host has learned for it. Which command is most directly useful?$q$,
 $q$["netstat", "ipconfig /release", "traceroute", "arp -a"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$arp -a lists the local ARP cache's current IP-to-MAC mappings, which is exactly the information needed to see which physical device a nearby host currently associates with the disputed IP. netstat lists connections, ipconfig /release drops a DHCP lease and is not useful for a static-IP conflict, and traceroute maps a path rather than local IP-to-MAC mappings.$q$),
('n-q-745','netplus',5,'5.4','mcq',1,
 $q$Which command lists the active TCP/UDP connections and listening ports on the local machine, useful for spotting an unexpected open port?$q$,
 $q$["netstat", "arp -a", "nslookup", "ipconfig"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$netstat reports the local host's active connections and listening ports, directly useful for investigating unexpected or suspicious open ports. arp -a reports IP-to-MAC mappings, nslookup queries DNS, and ipconfig shows local IP configuration - none of them list connection or port state.$q$),
('n-q-746','netplus',5,'5.4','multi',2,
 $q$Which TWO pieces of information can a Wi-Fi analyzer provide that a simple signal-bars icon cannot?$q$,
 $q$["Channel utilization and overlap among nearby access points", "The MAC address table stored on a wired switch", "The time-to-live value remaining on a DNS record", "The noise floor and signal-to-noise ratio at a given location"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 3]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$A Wi-Fi analyzer surveys the RF environment in detail, showing which channels are congested, how access points overlap, and the noise floor and signal-to-noise ratio at a location, well beyond a basic signal-strength icon. A switch's MAC address table and a DNS record's TTL are unrelated to wireless RF analysis.$q$),
('n-q-747','netplus',5,'5.4','multi',3,
 $q$Which TWO of the following are legitimate uses of tcpdump or a protocol analyzer during troubleshooting?$q$,
 $q$["Physically repairing a damaged network cable", "Confirming whether expected traffic is actually arriving at a host at the packet level", "Renewing a DHCP lease on the local host", "Diagnosing a malformed or unexpected field in a protocol handshake"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 3]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Packet capture tools are for observing and analyzing traffic on the wire - confirming the arrival of expected traffic and inspecting protocol-level details are core uses. They don't perform physical repairs or manage a DHCP lease; those require different tools entirely, such as a cable tester and ipconfig, respectively.$q$),
('n-q-748','netplus',5,'5.4','multi',2,
 $q$Which TWO things does a basic cable tester (wiremap tester) verify?$q$,
 $q$["That each pin is wired to the correct corresponding pin at the other end", "The cable's real-world sustained throughput under load", "The presence of opens, shorts, or reversed/split pairs", "The exact distance in meters to a break in the cable"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$A wiremap-style cable tester checks pin-to-pin continuity and common wiring faults like opens, shorts, and split or reversed pairs. Locating the precise distance to a fault requires a TDR, or an OTDR for fiber, and measuring actual sustained throughput requires a different kind of test entirely, not a basic continuity tester.$q$),
('n-q-749','netplus',5,'5.4','matching',2,
 $q$Match each command-line tool to what it primarily tells a technician.$q$,
 $q${"left": ["ping", "traceroute/tracert", "nslookup/dig", "arp -a", "netstat", "nmap"], "right": ["The active connections and listening ports on the local host", "Whether a host is reachable and its round-trip response time", "Which hosts are live and which ports are open across a range of addresses", "The hop-by-hop path to a destination and where along it latency or loss begins", "The IP address a hostname resolves to according to DNS", "The current IP-to-MAC address mappings learned by the local host"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 3, 4, 5, 0, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Each tool answers a distinct diagnostic question: ping tests reachability, traceroute maps the path, nslookup/dig resolves names via DNS, arp -a shows the local host's IP-to-MAC state, netstat shows the local host's connections, and nmap surveys many hosts and ports at once.$q$),
('n-q-750','netplus',5,'5.4','matching',2,
 $q$Match each physical/hardware troubleshooting tool to what it is used for.$q$,
 $q${"left": ["Toner probe (tone generator and probe)", "Cable tester (wiremap)", "OTDR", "Wi-Fi analyzer"], "right": ["Verifying correct pin-to-pin continuity and detecting opens, shorts, or split pairs", "Surveying channel utilization, overlap, and signal quality across the wireless spectrum", "Physically tracing and identifying which unlabeled cable in a bundle corresponds to a known jack", "Pinpointing the exact distance to a break or high-loss point in a fiber run"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 0, 3, 1]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$A toner probe traces a specific cable by sound, a wiremap tester checks wiring correctness, an OTDR locates a fiber fault by distance using light reflection, and a Wi-Fi analyzer surveys the RF environment - each solves a different physical-layer identification problem.$q$),
('n-q-751','netplus',5,'5.4','matching',3,
 $q$Match each tool output or result to what it means.$q$,
 $q${"left": ["ping reports \"Request timed out\" for every attempt", "tracert shows normal hops that suddenly become \"* * *\" for every hop from a point onward, including the destination", "nslookup returns \"NXDOMAIN\" for a hostname", "netstat shows a connection in the \"ESTABLISHED\" state"], "right": ["The DNS server has no record at all for that name, as opposed to a wrong or stale one", "A TCP connection has completed its handshake and is actively open between the two endpoints", "No reply was received within the timeout window - the destination may be down, filtered, or unreachable", "Traffic is being blocked or dropped starting at that point in the path, rather than one router simply ignoring probes"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2, 3, 0, 1]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$Interpreting tool output correctly is as important as knowing which tool to run: a single silent hop with a fully responsive destination afterward suggests one router ignoring probes, but timeouts continuing all the way to and including the destination suggest a real block; NXDOMAIN specifically means no record exists, distinguishing it from a resolvable-but-wrong answer; and ESTABLISHED in netstat confirms an active, completed TCP session rather than one still connecting or closing.$q$),
('n-q-752','netplus',5,'5.5','mcq',2,
 $q$Multiple users on the same floor intermittently receive IP addresses with the wrong subnet and default gateway for their location, even though the legitimate DHCP server's configuration is correct. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["A duplicate static IP address", "An unauthorized rogue DHCP server answering some client requests", "A DNS resolution failure", "A duplex mismatch on the switch"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$Wrong-but-valid-looking configuration handed out inconsistently, despite the real DHCP server being fine, is the signature of a second, unauthorized DHCP server racing to answer client requests first. A duplicate static IP wouldn't hand out an entire wrong subnet and gateway, and DNS or duplex issues don't affect DHCP-assigned addressing at all.$q$),
('n-q-753','netplus',5,'5.5','mcq',3,
 $q$A remote office can send traffic to a data center application, and the application logs show the requests arriving, but responses never reach the remote office, even though both sites show a healthy link to the internet. What should the technician suspect?$q$,
 $q$["A DNS resolution failure at the remote office", "An IP address conflict at the data center", "Asymmetric routing sending replies over a different path than requests, where a stateful device is dropping the return traffic", "A physical cable fault at the remote office"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,
 $q$Requests clearly arrive, proving the outbound path and DNS both work, but replies never return - a classic asymmetric-routing symptom where the return path differs from the request path and a stateful firewall or NAT device along that different path drops traffic it never saw the start of. A cable fault or DNS failure would prevent the request from arriving in the first place.$q$),
('n-q-754','netplus',5,'5.5','multi',2,
 $q$An internal web application works fine when accessed from within the building, but a specific TCP port used by a remote branch office is consistently refused, even though other branch traffic such as email and general browsing works normally. Which TWO checks point toward a host-based firewall on the application server as the cause, rather than a network-wide block?$q$,
 $q$["No users anywhere, including locally, can reach any service on that server", "Local users on the same server's subnet can still reach that port without issue", "Only that specific port is blocked while other ports/services on the same server remain reachable from the branch", "DNS lookups for the server's hostname fail company-wide"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 2]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$A host-based firewall rule blocking one port from certain sources still allows local traffic and other ports through - both symptoms point to a rule on the server itself rather than a company-wide network block. If literally nobody anywhere could reach the server, or if DNS itself were failing everywhere, that would suggest a much broader problem than one port from one location.$q$),
('n-q-755','netplus',5,'5.5','multi',2,
 $q$A newly added redundant switch-to-switch link, added without enabling spanning tree, causes the entire floor's network to become unusable within minutes. Which TWO symptoms would be consistent with the resulting broadcast storm?$q$,
 $q$["Only the two switches directly connected by the new link losing power", "Extremely high, sustained traffic utilization across the affected switches with no clear source", "A single user's IP address changing to an APIPA address", "Switch CPU and MAC address tables becoming overwhelmed, causing widespread flooding and slowness"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1, 3]$q$::jsonb,
 $q$A loop without spanning tree lets broadcast frames circulate and multiply, driving traffic utilization to saturation and overwhelming switch MAC tables and CPUs, degrading the entire connected segment, not just the two directly linked switches. Switches losing power or one user getting an APIPA address are unrelated failure modes, not signatures of a broadcast storm.$q$),
('n-q-756','netplus',5,'5.5','mcq',3,
 $q$Shortly after a server's system clock drifts several minutes out of sync with the rest of the domain, users begin receiving authentication failures when accessing domain resources, despite having correct credentials and full network connectivity. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["A DNS PTR record misconfiguration", "A duplicate IP address on the server", "An MTU mismatch on the server's NIC", "NTP time synchronization drift causing Kerberos ticket validation to fail"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,
 $q$Kerberos authentication depends on closely synchronized clocks between the client, server, and domain controller; once drift exceeds the tolerance, tickets are rejected as expired or invalid even though the network itself, DNS, and addressing are all fine. PTR records, duplicate IPs, and MTU mismatches don't produce authentication failures like this.$q$),
('n-q-757','netplus',5,'5.5','mcq',1,
 $q$External clients can reach a company's mail server by IP address, and forward DNS lookups for its hostname succeed correctly, but the mail server frequently gets flagged or rejected by receiving mail systems that perform a reverse-lookup check. What is the most likely cause?$q$,
 $q$["A missing or incorrect PTR record for the mail server's IP address", "A missing A record for the mail server's hostname", "A duplicate IP address on the mail server", "An expired TLS certificate on the mail server"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,
 $q$Reverse-lookup checks depend on a PTR record mapping the IP back to a hostname - forward resolution via the A record working fine doesn't guarantee the reverse mapping exists or is correct, and many mail systems specifically reject or flag messages that fail that check. A duplicate IP or a certificate issue would not specifically cause reverse-DNS validation failures.$q$),
('n-q-758','netplus',5,'5.5','mcq',1,
 $q$A server configured with two teamed NICs for redundancy stops passing traffic entirely after one of its two links begins experiencing a high error rate without ever going fully down. What does this suggest about the teaming configuration?$q$,
 $q$["The teaming software is functioning exactly as intended", "It failed to detect the degraded link as a failure and did not fail over to the healthy link", "The server has a duplicate IP address", "The switch port is on the wrong VLAN"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,
 $q$NIC teaming is meant to fail over when a link fails, but some configurations only watch for a link going fully down, not for a degraded link that is still technically up but performing poorly - so traffic keeps flowing over the bad link instead of moving to the healthy one, which is a teaming misconfiguration, not correct behavior. A duplicate IP or a VLAN issue would not specifically correlate with one NIC's rising error rate.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, domain, objective, deck, front, back) values
('n-f-700','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What five to six activities happen during the "identify the problem" step?$$,$$Gather information from the user, duplicate the problem if possible, question users about symptoms, identify the symptoms, determine if anything has changed recently, and approach multiple problems individually rather than assuming one cause.$$),
('n-f-701','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What is the "divide and conquer" troubleshooting approach?$$,$$Testing starts at a layer in the middle of the OSI model, often the network layer (e.g. checking IP connectivity), and the result determines whether to focus further testing on the layers above or the layers below that point.$$),
('n-f-702','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What's the difference between top-down and bottom-up OSI troubleshooting approaches?$$,$$Top-down starts at the application layer and works downward toward the physical layer; bottom-up starts at the physical layer (cables, link lights) and works upward toward the application. Both check every layer in order, unlike divide and conquer, which starts in the middle.$$),
('n-f-703','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$During "establish a theory of probable cause," why should a technician question the obvious first?$$,$$Simple, common causes (an unplugged cable, a disabled port, a powered-off device) are quick to check and frequently the actual cause - ruling them out early avoids wasting time chasing complex explanations for a basic problem.$$),
('n-f-704','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What happens once testing confirms a theory of probable cause is correct?$$,$$The technician moves to establishing a plan of action - determining the full fix and any potential side effects - before implementing the solution.$$),
('n-f-705','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What happens if testing does NOT confirm the theory?$$,$$The technician re-establishes a new theory based on what was learned and tests that instead; if no further theories can be formed, the issue is escalated to someone with more experience or access.$$),
('n-f-706','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$When should a technician escalate rather than keep forming new theories?$$,$$When their own theories have been tested and exhausted and the cause still isn't known - escalation brings in someone with deeper system access, vendor contacts, or specialized knowledge, rather than continuing to guess.$$),
('n-f-707','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What must a "plan of action" consider besides the fix itself?$$,$$Potential effects of the change - other services or users it might impact, whether a maintenance window is needed, and any rollback plan if the fix causes new problems.$$),
('n-f-708','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$Why verify full system functionality after implementing a fix, rather than just the originally reported symptom?$$,$$The fix, or the underlying cause, could affect other systems or users beyond the one that reported the problem, and confirming only the original symptom risks missing new issues introduced by the change.$$),
('n-f-709','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What are preventive measures in the troubleshooting methodology, and when are they added?$$,$$Actions taken during the verification step to stop the same problem from recurring, such as documentation, monitoring, or configuration hardening, added once the fix is confirmed to be fully working.$$),
('n-f-710','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What should documentation include after resolving a network issue?$$,$$The symptoms reported, the theory tested and confirmed, the actions taken to fix it, and the final outcome - creating a record that speeds up diagnosis of similar problems later and supports audits and shift handoffs.$$),
('n-f-711','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$Why should a technician duplicate the problem when possible during identification?$$,$$Reproducing the issue firsthand confirms exactly what is failing and under what conditions, rather than relying solely on a user's possibly incomplete or inaccurate description.$$),
('n-f-712','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$What does "determine if anything has changed" help identify during problem identification?$$,$$Recent changes (updates, configuration edits, new hardware, moved cables) are a common root cause, so checking what changed narrows the list of likely causes before a theory is even formed.$$),
('n-f-713','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$Why should a technician approach multiple, differently-symptomed reports as separate problems rather than assuming one shared cause?$$,$$Treating every report as connected can send the investigation down the wrong path if the symptoms actually stem from unrelated causes; each should be investigated individually until evidence ties them together.$$),
('n-f-714','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$Why is testing a theory a required step rather than jumping straight from a theory to implementing a fix?$$,$$An unconfirmed theory might be wrong; implementing a fix based on it risks making unnecessary changes, causing new problems, or leaving the actual root cause unaddressed - testing confirms the cause before committing to a fix.$$),
('n-f-715','netplus',5,'5.1','core',$$How does the methodology handle a case where testing reveals multiple confirmed causes needing separate fixes?$$,$$Each confirmed cause gets its own plan of action, implementation, and verification; problems are not lumped into a single fix just because they were investigated together, since each needs to be verified independently as resolved.$$),
('n-f-716','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What is attenuation and what causes it?$$,$$The gradual loss of signal strength as it travels over a cable or through the air; caused by cable length beyond spec, cable quality or damage, or, for wireless, distance and obstructions. Longer runs and lower-grade cable increase it.$$),
('n-f-717','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What is crosstalk, and how does twisted-pair cabling minimize it?$$,$$Crosstalk is unwanted signal coupling from one wire pair onto an adjacent pair. Twisting each pair at a different rate causes the induced interference to cancel out; excessive untwisting at termination or cable damage can defeat this and reintroduce crosstalk.$$),
('n-f-718','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What's the difference between a straight-through and a crossover cable, pin-wise?$$,$$A straight-through cable wires each pin to the same pin at both ends, used to connect unlike devices (e.g., PC to switch). A crossover cable swaps the transmit and receive pairs, used to connect two like devices (e.g., switch to switch) that lack auto-MDI-X.$$),
('n-f-719','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What is a split-pair wiring fault, and why does it pass a basic continuity test?$$,$$A split pair connects wires from two different twisted pairs to the pins of a single logical pair. End-to-end continuity is intact pin-for-pin, so a simple continuity/wiremap tester reports success, but the signal no longer benefits from the pair's twist-induced noise cancellation, causing crosstalk and errors.$$),
('n-f-720','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What symptom distinguishes a bad connector from a bad cable run?$$,$$A bad connector typically causes connectivity to drop or become intermittent specifically when the connector is touched, bumped, or under physical strain, since the fault is at the mechanical connection point rather than distributed along the cable's length.$$),
('n-f-721','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$Why do cables have a minimum bend radius, and what happens if it's exceeded?$$,$$Bending a cable tighter than its rated minimum radius stresses the internal conductors (copper) or glass core (fiber), increasing attenuation and, over time, risking permanent damage or breakage at the bend point.$$),
('n-f-722','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What happens electrically if TX and RX are transposed between two connected devices, and how does auto-MDI-X prevent it?$$,$$If both devices expect to receive on the same pins the other is also expecting to receive on, no valid signal path exists and the link fails to come up. Auto-MDI-X automatically detects this and electrically swaps the pair assignment on one side, letting either a straight-through or crossover cable work between any two ports.$$),
('n-f-723','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What are the symptoms of a duplex mismatch?$$,$$The link comes up and shows a light, but the half-duplex side reports late collisions while the full-duplex side reports no collisions at all - throughput is degraded even though basic connectivity works.$$),
('n-f-724','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What are the symptoms of a speed mismatch or unstable auto-negotiation between a NIC and switch port?$$,$$Inconsistent or unexpectedly low link speed, occasional link drops during renegotiation, and CRC/frame errors - often traced to one side forced to a fixed speed/duplex while the other is left on auto-negotiate.$$),
('n-f-725','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$Does a lit link light guarantee good performance? Why or why not?$$,$$No - a link light only confirms that the physical link came up and basic negotiation succeeded. It says nothing about ongoing error rates, so a marginal, damaged, or poorly terminated cable can still show a solid link light while causing slow, error-prone performance.$$),
('n-f-726','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$Why does a dirty or scratched fiber-optic connector end-face cause signal loss?$$,$$Dust, oil, and scratches on the polished end-face scatter or block the light signal as it crosses the connection point, increasing attenuation - one of the most common real-world causes of degraded fiber links, and one fixed with a simple cleaning tool rather than replacing hardware.$$),
('n-f-727','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What happens if a single-mode SFP transceiver is used on a multimode fiber run, or vice versa?$$,$$The transceiver's optics are designed for one fiber core size and light-propagation mode; mismatching them causes severe coupling loss, very limited range, or a link that fails to establish at all, even if both ends use identical mismatched transceivers.$$),
('n-f-728','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$Why does running Cat5e cable on a link meant for 10GbE typically cause the link to negotiate down or fail, rather than just being slightly slower?$$,$$Cat5e is not certified for 10GbE's higher signaling frequency and stricter crosstalk/attenuation limits, especially at longer lengths; the link commonly auto-negotiates down to a supported speed like 1 Gbps, or shows excessive errors if it does attempt 10GbE.$$),
('n-f-729','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What's the difference between near-end crosstalk (NEXT) and far-end crosstalk (FEXT)?$$,$$NEXT is crosstalk measured at the same end of the cable where the interfering signal originates (strongest, since the aggressor signal hasn't yet attenuated); FEXT is measured at the opposite end, after the aggressor signal has traveled the cable's length and weakened.$$),
('n-f-730','netplus',5,'5.2','core',$$What does a cracked or worn RJ45 connector retention clip typically cause?$$,$$Intermittent disconnects whenever the cable is bumped, flexed, or under tension, since the clip can no longer hold the connector fully seated in the jack - a mechanical fault distinct from an electrical wiring problem.$$),
('n-f-731','netplus',5,'5.2','acronym',$$EMI$$,$$Electromagnetic Interference - noise coupled onto cabling from nearby electrical sources (motors, ballasts, power lines); mitigated with shielded cable, distance, or rerouting away from the source.$$),
('n-f-732','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What is network congestion, and what symptom does it typically cause?$$,$$Congestion occurs when traffic demand exceeds available link capacity at some point in the path; it typically causes increased latency, jitter, and eventually packet loss as buffers overflow, especially noticeable during peak usage periods.$$),
('n-f-733','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What is a network bottleneck?$$,$$The single slowest or lowest-capacity link or device along a path, which caps the effective throughput of the entire path regardless of how fast the other segments are - for example, a single gigabit uplink serving many gigabit access ports.$$),
('n-f-734','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What's the difference between latency and jitter?$$,$$Latency is how long a packet takes to arrive; jitter is how much that delay varies between consecutive packets. A link can have high latency with no jitter (consistent delay) or low latency with high jitter (erratic delay) - each is diagnosed and treated differently.$$),
('n-f-735','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What is packet loss, and how is it typically identified?$$,$$Packets that are sent but never arrive, usually from congestion, faulty hardware, or a poor wireless signal; identified by tools like ping or traceroute reporting missing replies or timeouts rather than merely slow ones.$$),
('n-f-736','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What is bandwidth saturation, and how does it differ from latency?$$,$$Saturation means a link's total available capacity is fully consumed by current traffic; it's a capacity problem, whereas latency is a delay problem - a saturated but otherwise healthy link often shows increasing latency and jitter as a downstream symptom, but the root cause is insufficient capacity.$$),
('n-f-737','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What causes co-channel interference in Wi-Fi, and how is it fixed?$$,$$Multiple access points within range of each other are set to the same channel, forcing them to take turns transmitting; the fix is assigning non-overlapping channels, such as 1, 6, and 11 on 2.4GHz, to nearby APs, or moving some to 5GHz.$$),
('n-f-738','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$Why does high client/channel density degrade Wi-Fi performance even when signal strength is strong?$$,$$Wireless is a shared medium - every associated client contends for the same limited airtime on an access point. A large number of simultaneous devices causes contention and delay regardless of how strong each individual device's signal is.$$),
('n-f-739','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What causes insufficient wireless coverage, and what's the typical fix?$$,$$Areas too far from the nearest access point, or blocked by obstructions such as walls, metal, or distance, experience weak signal and drops; the fix is adding access points or repeaters, or adjusting AP placement/power to close the coverage gap.$$),
('n-f-740','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$How do non-Wi-Fi devices like microwave ovens or cordless phones interfere with wireless networks?$$,$$Devices operating in the same general RF range as 2.4GHz Wi-Fi, such as microwaves, some cordless phones, and Bluetooth, can leak enough energy to disrupt nearby wireless traffic; moving affected clients/APs to the 5GHz band, which those devices don't share, avoids the conflict.$$),
('n-f-741','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What is QoS, and how does it help with jitter and congestion on a shared link?$$,$$Quality of Service classifies and prioritizes traffic so latency-sensitive types (voice, video) are transmitted ahead of less time-sensitive traffic (bulk downloads) when a link is congested, reducing the delay variation that causes choppy real-time audio/video.$$),
('n-f-742','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$How does an MTU mismatch along a path cause performance problems?$$,$$If a device forwards a packet larger than the next hop's MTU and fragmentation is not permitted, the packet is dropped rather than fragmented, causing failed transfers or silently degraded performance for protocols that don't handle this gracefully - commonly seen with tunneled/VPN traffic.$$),
('n-f-743','netplus',5,'5.3','core',$$What is bufferbloat, and how does it cause latency spikes under load?$$,$$Bufferbloat happens when a network device, often a home router, has oversized buffers that queue traffic instead of dropping it when a link saturates; packets wait in the deep queue rather than being dropped, so latency balloons under load even though no packets are technically lost.$$),
('n-f-744','netplus',5,'5.3','acronym',$$MTU$$,$$Maximum Transmission Unit - the largest packet size a network link will forward without fragmentation; a common Ethernet MTU is 1500 bytes.$$),
('n-f-745','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What does the ping command test, and what protocol does it use?$$,$$It tests basic reachability and round-trip time to a destination by sending ICMP echo request packets and waiting for echo reply packets; success confirms only that ICMP traffic can reach and return from that host, not that every service on it works.$$),
('n-f-746','netplus',5,'5.4','acronym',$$ICMP$$,$$Internet Control Message Protocol - carries diagnostic and error messages, like ping's echo request/reply and "destination unreachable," rather than application data.$$),
('n-f-747','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$How does traceroute/tracert use TTL to map the path to a destination?$$,$$It sends probes with a progressively increasing TTL, starting at 1; each router that discards a probe because its TTL hit zero sends back a "time exceeded" message identifying itself, letting traceroute build the hop-by-hop path one router at a time.$$),
('n-f-748','netplus',5,'5.4','acronym',$$TTL$$,$$Time to Live - a counter decremented by each router a packet passes through; when it reaches zero the packet is discarded, preventing routing loops from circulating packets forever.$$),
('n-f-749','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What's the difference between nslookup and dig for DNS troubleshooting?$$,$$Both query DNS servers directly to check name resolution. dig, Linux/macOS-oriented but also available on Windows, typically shows more detailed output (full record data, timing, flags) in a single query, while nslookup is simpler and available by default on Windows and most Unix-like systems.$$),
('n-f-750','netplus',5,'5.4','acronym',$$DNS$$,$$Domain Name System - resolves human-readable hostnames to IP addresses (and vice versa); troubleshot with nslookup/dig and diagnosed by comparing IP-based versus name-based connectivity.$$),
('n-f-751','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What information does ipconfig /all (or ifconfig/ip addr) show that a plain ipconfig doesn't?$$,$$Additional detail such as the MAC address, DHCP lease times, DNS server addresses, and whether DHCP is enabled - useful when the basic IP/gateway/mask summary isn't enough to diagnose an addressing problem.$$),
('n-f-752','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What do ipconfig /release and ipconfig /renew do, and when would you use them?$$,$$/release gives up the current DHCP-assigned IP address; /renew requests a new one from the DHCP server. Used together to force a client to re-request an address, which can resolve DHCP-related issues like a stale, conflicting, or APIPA address.$$),
('n-f-753','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What does arp -a show, and what troubleshooting scenario is it useful for?$$,$$The local ARP cache's current IP-to-MAC address mappings; useful for confirming which physical device a host associates with a given IP, such as investigating a suspected duplicate IP address or a device that's been replaced but not updated.$$),
('n-f-754','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What does netstat show, and what's a common use case?$$,$$The local host's active network connections and listening ports; commonly used to check whether an expected service is listening, or to spot an unexpected open port/connection that might indicate malware or a misconfiguration.$$),
('n-f-755','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What's the difference between tcpdump and a full protocol analyzer like Wireshark?$$,$$tcpdump is a lightweight, command-line packet capture tool, often used to capture traffic on a server or remote device with no GUI; a full protocol analyzer like Wireshark adds a graphical interface with deep protocol decoding, filtering, and analysis, often used to open a capture file that tcpdump produced.$$),
('n-f-756','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What does nmap do, and why must its use be authorized?$$,$$nmap scans a range of addresses to discover live hosts and open ports/services, useful for auditing a network's exposed attack surface. Scanning networks without authorization can be indistinguishable from reconnaissance for an attack and may violate policy or law, so it should only be run with explicit permission.$$),
('n-f-757','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What does a basic cable tester (wiremap tester) check that a toner probe does not?$$,$$A wiremap tester verifies that each pin is correctly wired end-to-end and flags opens, shorts, and split/reversed pairs. A toner probe doesn't test wiring correctness at all - it's used purely to physically locate and identify which cable in a bundle corresponds to a given jack.$$),
('n-f-758','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What is a toner probe used for?$$,$$Identifying a specific, otherwise-unlabeled cable within a bundle or wiring closet: a tone generator injects an audible signal at one end, and a probe detects that tone at the other end or along the cable's length, letting a technician trace it physically.$$),
('n-f-759','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What's the difference between a TDR and an OTDR?$$,$$A TDR (Time Domain Reflectometer) tests copper cable, sending an electrical pulse and timing its reflection off a fault to calculate distance. An OTDR does the same using light pulses over fiber-optic cable, also reporting distance to a break or high-loss point.$$),
('n-f-760','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$What does a Wi-Fi analyzer show that a device's basic signal-strength icon doesn't?$$,$$Detailed RF environment data: channel utilization and overlap among nearby access points, noise floor, signal-to-noise ratio, and which channels are congested - information needed to properly place APs or pick non-overlapping channels.$$),
('n-f-761','netplus',5,'5.4','core',$$Why does "ping succeeds by IP but fails by hostname" point specifically at DNS rather than routing?$$,$$A successful ping by IP already proves the network path, routing, and basic reachability all work; if the same destination fails only when addressed by name, the one added variable is name resolution, isolating the fault to DNS rather than anything downstream of it.$$),
('n-f-762','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What is a rogue DHCP server, and what symptoms does it cause?$$,$$An unauthorized DHCP server on the network that hands out its own, often incorrect, IP addresses, gateways, or DNS servers; affected clients get seemingly valid but wrong configuration, causing intermittent connectivity that appears random because it depends on which server answered first.$$),
('n-f-763','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What is asymmetric routing, and why can it cause intermittent connectivity or dropped stateful connections?$$,$$Traffic to a destination takes one path while replies return via a different path. Stateful devices along one of those paths, like a firewall, may never see the full conversation and drop it, causing connections that work briefly then fail, or fail only in one direction.$$),
('n-f-764','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What's the difference between a network firewall and a host-based firewall blocking a specific port?$$,$$A network firewall blocks traffic for everyone passing through it, so the service would appear down to all remote users; a host-based firewall on the destination machine blocks only that machine, so local access still works while remote connections to that one port fail.$$),
('n-f-765','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What causes a broadcast storm, and how does STP prevent it?$$,$$An accidental switching loop, a redundant physical path between switches with no loop prevention, lets broadcast frames circulate and multiply endlessly, overwhelming the network. Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) detects redundant paths and blocks one of them, keeping only a single active path between any two switches.$$),
('n-f-766','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$Why can NTP time drift break Kerberos authentication or certificate validation across a domain?$$,$$Kerberos tickets and certificate validity windows depend on closely synchronized clocks; if a device's clock drifts too far from the domain's time source, tickets appear expired or not-yet-valid and authentication fails, even though the network itself is functioning normally.$$),
('n-f-767','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What is a PTR record, and what breaks when it's missing or incorrect?$$,$$A PTR record maps an IP address back to a hostname, the opposite of an A record. Missing or wrong PTR records can cause reverse-lookup failures, which some mail servers and logging/authentication systems rely on, even though forward name-to-IP lookups work fine.$$),
('n-f-768','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What is NIC teaming/failover, and what happens when it fails to detect a degraded (not fully down) link?$$,$$NIC teaming combines multiple network adapters for redundancy or throughput, automatically failing over if one link goes down. If a link degrades (high errors, reduced speed) without going fully down, some teaming configurations don't detect the problem and keep sending traffic over the bad link instead of failing over.$$),
('n-f-769','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What is a black-hole route, and what symptom does it cause?$$,$$A route that accepts and forwards traffic toward a destination that is actually unreachable from that path, silently discarding it with no error returned. It causes connections to hang and time out rather than fail immediately with a clear "unreachable" message.$$),
('n-f-770','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$Why might a multi-homed host with two default gateways experience intermittent connectivity?$$,$$A host should have only one active default gateway; if two are configured, outbound traffic can unpredictably use whichever gateway the OS currently prefers, and if that gateway doesn't have a route back to the source network, replies are lost - causing connectivity that seems to work sometimes and fail other times.$$),
('n-f-771','netplus',5,'5.5','core',$$What is a routing loop, and what symptom in a traceroute reveals it?$$,$$A routing loop occurs when two or more routers each list the other as the next hop toward a destination, so a packet circulates until its TTL reaches zero. A traceroute reveals this as the same set of hop addresses repeating over and over instead of progressing toward the destination.$$);
